\begin{lemma}\label{lem:union}
Let $a,b,c$ be involutions acting on a finite set $\Omega$ on the right.
Put $K=\gen{ab,bc}$ and $H=\gen{a,b,c}$. If a nonempty subset $T$ is
preserved by $ab$ and $bc$, then $U=T\cup T^b$ is $H$-invariant and
$0<|U|\leq 2|T|$. In particular, if $G$ acts transitively on $\Omega$,
$H\leq G$, and $2|T|<|\Omega|$, then $H$ is a proper subgroup of $G$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The restrictions of the permutations $ab$ and $bc$ to the finite set $T$
are bijections: containment of their images in $T$ implies equality by
cardinality. Their inverses consequently preserve $T$, and hence $T$ is
$K$-invariant. Conjugation by $b$ sends $ab$ to $ba=(ab)^{-1}$ and $bc$
to $cb=(bc)^{-1}$. Thus $b$ normalizes $K$. Moreover,
$a=(ab)b$ and $c=b(bc)$, so $H=\gen{K,b}$.

For $k\in K$, the identity $bk=(bkb)b$, together with $bkb\in K$, gives
$(T^b)^k=(T^{bkb})^b=T^b$. Both $T$ and $T^b$ are therefore preserved
by $K$, while $b$ interchanges them. It follows that $H$ preserves their
union. Its cardinality satisfies $|T|\leq |U|\leq 2|T|$.
Under the final hypotheses $U$ is a nonempty proper $H$-invariant subset.
If $H=G$, it would also be $G$-invariant, contrary to transitivity.
\end{proof}

